ID: 62e1195c-316f-45f3-819d-6e5575f41c13
Comment: In `ScanlineRendererImpl::StartUsing`, removing the `UI.system-fog-of-war` check means existing `ScanlineControl` instances will still activate the scanline shader even after fog-of-war is turned off. Since `ScanlineControl::Render()` no longer guards on the option, this can leave scanlines visible when the setting is disabled at runtime.

ID: 0d97b5a1-73fa-4eb1-9561-ec7b1741ca7e
Comment: `StartUsing()` no longer checks `UI.system-fog-of-war`, but `ScanlineControl::Render()` calls the renderer unconditionally. Controls created while fog-of-war was enabled can therefore continue drawing scanlines after the option is disabled. Please retain the option check here or add it to `ScanlineControl::Render()` (and any other unguarded callers).

ID: 80930976-f0d4-4d4c-9af8-f947f74e481c
Comment: Removing the `!GetOptionsDB().Get<bool>("UI.system-fog-of-war")` check from `ScanlineRendererImpl::StartUsing()` shifts the feature-gating responsibility to every call site. While current callers guard this check, relying on them makes the method's implicit preconditions fragile. If a future caller invokes `StartUsing()` without checking the option, it will eagerly compile and bind the shader unnecessarily. It is better design to keep the guard inside `StartUsing()` to prevent unwanted initialization, or to centralize the check by removing it from all callers and letting the renderer handle the default toggle state.

