ID: 4700475e-a7d4-49f8-b117-e347cab3b82a
Comment: `redirect(str_replace('://', '', $this->input->post('redirect')))` is not a safe guard against external redirects. A value like `//evil.com` (or other non-`://` absolute forms) will pass unchanged and may still be treated as an external Location by `redirect()`. Can we enforce a strictly internal path (e.g., allow only relative URIs or validate against `site_url` host) before redirecting?

ID: 65160707-a4d3-46a6-aeb7-c645be25faa2
Comment: The use of `str_replace('://', '', $this->input->post('redirect'))` to remove protocol prefixes seems fragile and incomplete. It only removes '://' but doesn't handle cases where 'https://' or any other protocol with '://' is in the URL. A more robust approach would be to use a proper URL parsing function or library if available in the framework.

ID: e7376762-01ca-484e-adcf-d684415ddfd6
Comment: `str_replace('://', '', $this->input->post('redirect'))` will mangle any absolute URL before passing it to `redirect()`, so a value like `http://example.com/foo` becomes `httpexample.com/foo` rather than a valid destination. If the goal is to prevent external redirects, this needs explicit validation/whitelisting instead of a string replace.

ID: ff582921-2a50-4c93-b1f1-b735fef78c37
Comment: The use of `str_replace('://', '', $this->input->post('redirect'))` will incorrectly mangle absolute URLs (e.g., `http://example.com` becomes `http:example.com`), causing the redirect to fail or navigate to an invalid route. Consider validating that the redirect value is a relative path or belongs to the same domain instead of stripping the protocol.

ID: 5c6bb746-6391-421c-a7c8-2656f5064323
Comment: The comment 'Go to the redirect if any' is now slightly misleading as it implies the redirect might exist when it's just a POSTed value. Consider clarifying the comment to reflect that it's checking for a POSTed redirect parameter.

ID: 4e83e561-77ea-41ff-88c6-b97c2dfe5b21
Comment: Using 'str_replace' to remove '://' in redirect URLs could lead to unexpected behavior and security issues, such as redirecting to an unintended destination. Could you clarify the reasoning behind this change and whether it's necessary?

ID: 033c95f6-0580-4404-87c9-feef85765c90
Comment: `str_replace('://', '', ...)` does not prevent external redirects: a submitted protocol-relative URL such as `//evil.example` contains no `://` and may still be emitted by `redirect()` as an off-site `Location`. Please validate that the target is an internal path (ideally an allowed admin URI) rather than trying to sanitize schemes by string replacement.

ID: f0ef9719-1c60-43ad-9e56-b94367281e52
Comment: The use of `str_replace('://', '', ...)` to sanitize the redirect URL is an incorrect way to prevent open redirects. It fails to block protocol-relative URLs (e.g., `//attacker.com`) and mangles valid absolute URLs (e.g., `https://example.com` becomes `httpsexample.com`). A more robust approach would be to validate that the redirect path begins with a single `/`.

