ID: e2634500-b5b0-4353-819e-febe1d2a5cd4
Comment: The code change removes the line `$this->productChangeMessageProducer->productChanged($productForRecalculations);` from the loop in the `delete` method. This could lead to a loss of functionality if the intent was to notify about changes for products scheduled for recalculations. Please confirm if this change is intentional.

ID: d814e637-1580-4a48-8813-cc871dddeec3
Comment: Removing the call to `productChanged($productForRecalculations)` within the loop in `delete()` might lead to stale search indices. While the single call to `productChanged($product)` is intended to cover relevant changes, it only handles the immediate associates (variants/main variant) of the deleted product. If `$productsForRecalculations` contains products that are not part of that direct relationship, they will not be re-indexed.

ID: 5b08e2db-a813-4bbd-8fa6-28aa62cb79eb
Comment: In `ProductFacade::delete()`, removing `productChanged($productForRecalculations)` from the `foreach ($productsForRecalculations ...)` loop may skip Elasticsearch updates for products affected by the deletion but not directly associated with `$product` (main/variant relation). We still publish only `productChanged($product)` afterward, and `ProductChangeMessageProducer::productChanged()` expands just the deleted product’s associated IDs. Can we confirm `getProductsForRecalculations()` is always a subset of those associated IDs? If not, this is a functional regression.

ID: 829fa2a2-42d6-47e2-b623-e94bce918ce7
Comment: In `productsChangedByIds`, the `$this->scheduledProductIds` property is overwritten in each iteration of the loop instead of being appended to. This means that only the associated IDs of the last product in the array (plus the input IDs) will be kept, potentially causing other affected products to be missed in the search index update.

ID: 659e231c-5912-4c04-9279-3372b70586ea
Comment: Does removing `productChanged($productForRecalculations)` from within the `foreach` loop guarantee that all products currently being recalculated will still trigger the correct Elasticsearch updates? While calling it on the main `$product` afterward likely covers associated variants/children via `getAllAssociatedProductIds`, it's worth verifying that `$productsForRecalculations` exclusively contains products linked to `$product`. If other unrelated products are included here, they might now miss the index update.

ID: 5990da03-bd51-4da5-988c-353c6eb631df
Comment: Please keep notifying changes for each `$productForRecalculations` (or batch them with `productsChanged($productsForRecalculations)`). The remaining `productChanged($product)` only queues the deleted product and its variant associations, while `getProductsForRecalculations()` may contain other products whose recalculated price/availability must also be re-exported. Removing this call can leave those products stale in Elasticsearch.

